%n [arūru | iss]<û> | ahātam
#note: See @bib{RN117@291}: “The emendation follows the later text: @i{Arūru issû rabītu} (SB I 94). Aruru’s title of ‘sister’ is documented for Sumerian and bilingual texts by @bib{RN625@74} (add @i{Lugale} 413); there she is typically the (elder) sister of Enlil. In Babylonian texts the Mother Goddess is ‘sister’ of all the gods: SB Anzû I 167 // 172, quoted above; @bib{soden1978erste@68}, OB Atram-ḫasīs 296: @akk{{d}ma-mi [a-h]a?-su₂-nu} (cf. @bib{RN809@192}); note also, in another account of the creation of man, @akk{{d}be-let-DINGIR{meš} NIN DINGIR{meš} GAL{meš}} (@bib{RN357@56, 11′, 31′}), where it is again Ea who so addresses her.“ 
// L I.4 SB "I" 92

%n [š]ū | rīmum-ma (||) ur[uk | supūri]
#note: See @bib{fromebllab0027@137}: “The interpretation of this line follows a suggestion by A. R. George, who understands it as a variant of the first millennium version: @akk{šu-u₂ SIPA-ma ša₂ UNUG{ki} su-pu₂-ru}, “he who is shepherd of Uruk-the-Sheepfold!” The variation between @i{rīmu}, “wild bull,” and @i{rēʾû}, “shepherd,” is probably motivated or facilitated by phonetic factors (VwV is written as VmV already in some Old Babylonian texts).”
// L I.4 SB "I" 85

%n [...] | rigm[a? | ...]
#note: See @bib{fromebllab0027@137}: “Compare MB Ug₁ 14–15 (the first line is also preserved in SB I 76): @i{tazzimtašina iltenemme ištar} / @i{rigmu marṣu iktanaššada ana šamê āni}, “Ištar keeps hearing their complaint, / the terrible din reaching (even) the heaven of Anu.””
// cf. L I.4 MB "Ug₁" 15
